# Why are some firms so much more productive than others?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How large are the productivity gaps within one industry? What did Bloom and Van Reenen find about management?"
next: {"title":"Where do new firms come from, and why do most die?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/119-where-do-new-firms-come-from-and-why-do.cast"}

## Twice the output
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '0.5'
      - '0.6'
      - '0.7'
      - '0.8'
      - '0.9'
      - '1.0'
      - '1.1'
      - '1.2'
      - '1.3'
      - '1.4'
      - '1.5'
      - '1.6'
      - '1.7'
      - '1.8'
    values:
      - 0.8
      - 3.6
      - 8.4
      - 13.2
      - 15.9
      - 15.6
      - 13.3
      - 10.1
      - 7.1
      - 4.7
      - 3
      - 1.8
      - 1.1
      - 0.6
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: '% of plants'
    x_label: Productivity A (median plant = 1)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 18
    gap: 0.2
    label_size: 18
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 360
      w: 820
      h: 280
sources:
    - id: syverson2011
      title: What Determines Productivity?
      authors: C. Syverson
      year: 2011
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Literature 49(2): within 4-digit SIC US manufacturing industries the average 90th–10th percentile log TFP gap is 0.651, a ratio of 1.92 (from Syverson 2004); Hsieh and Klenow find average 90-10 ratios over 5:1 in China and India; year-to-year autocorrelation of plant TFP 0.6–0.8.'
    - id: syverson2004
      title: Product Substitutability and Productivity Dispersion
      authors: C. Syverson
      year: 2004
      finding: 'Review of Economics and Statistics 86(2): 90-10 TFP ratio within US 4-digit manufacturing industries averages about 1.9.'
    - id: hsiehklenow2009
      title: Misallocation and Manufacturing TFP in China and India
      authors: C.-T. Hsieh, P. J. Klenow
      year: 2009
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 124(4): productivity dispersion across plants much larger in China and India than in the US.'

    text hunch "competition: the weak exit?" #8f887c x 690 y 580 font_size 28 hidden true
    math def tex "A = \\frac{\\text{output}}{\\text{all inputs}}" x 760 y 560 hidden true
    label t10 "10th" above #b5482e attach_to bar_3 font_size 24 hidden true
    label t90 "90th" above #2f6b8f attach_to bar_10 font_size 24 hidden true
    scale ratio x 170 y 185 width 660 label "top ÷ bottom" min 1 max 6 value 1.9 ticks 5 hidden true
    text us "US ≈ 1.9×" #2f6b8f cites ["syverson2011","syverson2004"] x 300 y 272 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ci "China, India > 5×\n(revenue measure)" #b5482e cites ["hsiehklenow2009","syverson2011"] x 740 y 248 font_size 28 hidden true
    text persist "persistent: this year ≈ next year" #3d3833 cites ["syverson2011"] x 690 y 590 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Twice the output"

Same industry, same workers, same machines. Why would one factory get far more out of them than the one down the road?
    draw axes

B: Surely it couldn't. In a (@draw hunch@) competitive market the weak one should be gone by now.

    erase hunch

That's the textbook hunch. To test it, measure productivity: out (@draw def@)put per unit of all the inputs together, labour, machines, materials. Economists call it total factor productivity, A.

    erase def

Here are Ame (@draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6 bar_7 bar_8 bar_9 bar_10 bar_11 bar_12 bar_13 bar_14 parallel true@)rican factories inside one narrow industry, ranked by A. The smooth shape is a sketch; the spread is real.

B: Take the plant at the ten (@draw t10@)th percentile, near the bottom, and the one a (@draw t90@)t the ninetieth, near the top.

    draw ratio

    ask "Click the line at your guess: the top plant's output, as a multiple of the bottom one's." on ratio store g default "3.5" after keep
        intro: From the same inputs, how many times as much output does the top plant make?
        right: One point nine: you said {g:1}, close.
        wrong: You said {g:1}: here, one point nine.

Almost twice (@draw us@) as much: one point nine. That's Chad Syverson's average across the narrow industries of American manufacturing.

B: Twice the output from the same inputs. And America is the tidy case?
    highlight us

Yes. In China and India, Chang-Tai Hsieh and Peter Klenow used a different measure, built fr (@draw ci@)om revenue, and found the gap above five to one, well above America's on that same measure.

    pause 0.4

B: Maybe the top plants just had a lucky year.
    highlight bar_10 effect pulse

No: a plant's productivity this year stron (@draw persist@)gly predicts next year's. The gaps last. So what do the top plants know that the bottom ones don't?

## Does management matter?
use: did_trends
with:
    effect: 0
    treat_at: 4
    group_labels:
      - Consulted
      - Not consulted
    y_label: Productivity
    show_counterfactual: true
sources:
    - id: bloomvr2007
      title: Measuring and Explaining Management Practices Across Firms and Countries
      authors: N. Bloom, J. Van Reenen
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 122(4): phone interviews with plant managers in 700+ medium-sized manufacturing firms in the US, UK, France and Germany scoring 18 practices (operations, monitoring, targets, incentives) 1–5; better scores go with higher productivity, profitability, Tobin''s q, sales growth and survival.'
    - id: bloom2013
      title: Does Management Matter? Evidence from India
      authors: N. Bloom, B. Eifert, A. Mahajan, D. McKenzie, J. Roberts
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 128(1): 17 large textile firms (28 plants) near Mumbai randomised in 2008, 11 treated with intensive consulting; productivity rose 17% in the first year through better quality, efficiency and lower inventory; informational barriers explained non-adoption.'
    - id: jpal
      title: Increasing Firm Productivity through Management Consulting Services in India (evaluation summary)
      authors: J-PAL
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Treatment: about four months of hands-on consulting after a one-month diagnostic that both groups got; quality defects fell by about half; productivity +16.6% in the first year; estimated profit gain about US$325,000 per plant per year; firms often did not know the practices or did not believe they would pay.'

    scratch sc cites ["bloomvr2007"] work ["Bloom & Van Reenen, 2007","700+ factories: US, UK, France, Germany","18 practices, scored 1–5","defects? targets? promotions?","well run → more productive"] x 500 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    label tag17 "+17%" left #b5482e attach_to effect_arrow cites ["bloom2013"] font_size 40 hidden true
    text defects "defects −50%" #3d3833 cites ["jpal"] x 640 y 265 font_size 32 hidden true
    text money "≈ $325,000 a year per plant" #3d3833 cites ["jpal"] x 640 y 265 font_size 32 hidden true
    text why "didn't know · didn't believe" #8a5fa8 cites ["jpal","bloom2013"] x 640 y 265 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Does management matter?"

What do the top plants know? Nick Bloom and John Van Reenen decided simply to ask the managers.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

B: Their team phoned managers at more than seven hundred mid-sized factories in America, Britain, France and Germany.
    draw sc_line_2

B: They scored eighteen practices from one to five. Do you tra (@draw sc_line_4@)ck defects? Do you set targets, and does anyone check them?
    draw sc_line_3

B: Are people promoted for performance, or for staying long? The well-run f (@draw sc_line_5@)irms were more productive, more profitable, and more likely to survive.

But that's a correlation. Maybe successful firms can simply afford tidy management.
    highlight sc_line_5 effect underline

    erase sc

B: So Bloom and colle (@draw axes treat_line@)agues ran an experiment in 2008: seventeen big textile firms near Mumbai.

B: Eleven, picked at ra (@draw treated_line control_line@)ndom, got four months of hands-on consulting; the other six only a first check-up.

Before the consultants arrived, both groups ran on the same track. Your guess first.
    highlight treated_line control_line

    quiz "Within a year, how much did productivity rise in the consulted plants, against the others?" judge false
        right: Here's what they found.
        * About 2%
        * About 17%
        * About 50%

Seventeen percent, within the first year. The consulted plants pulled away from the path they were on.
    animate effect 1.7 duration 2.5

    draw counterfactual effect_arrow tag17

B: Quality defects fell  (@draw defects@)by about half. And none of it was a new machine: they measured defects, logged downtime, cleared old stock off the floor.

    erase defects

Worth an estimated three (@draw money@) hundred and twenty-five thousand dollars a year per plant. Then why hadn't they done it already?

    erase money

B: Most owners didn't know the practic (@draw why@)es existed, or didn't believe they would pay. And nothing forced them to find out.

## Who gets the workers
vars:
    m:
      value: 10
      min: 0
      max: 20
      label: Bakers at Dockside
    avg:
      expr: (100*m + 50*(20-m))/20
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'Dockside: 100 loaves'
      - 'Seaview: 50 loaves'
    values:
      - 10
      - 10
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: ' bakers'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - green
      - grey
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 20
    y_label: Bakers (of 20)
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 190
      w: 400
      h: 430
bind_params: {"values.0":"m","values.1":"20-m"}
sources:
    - id: bhs2013
      title: 'Cross-Country Differences in Productivity: The Role of Allocation and Selection'
      authors: E. Bartelsman, J. Haltiwanger, S. Scarpetta
      year: 2013
      finding: 'American Economic Review 103(1): the Olley–Pakes covariance term for labour productivity averages about 50 log points in US manufacturing industries (industry productivity about 50% higher than with employment shares allocated at random), 20–30 in Western Europe, and close to zero or negative in Central and Eastern Europe early in the transition, rising substantially during the 1990s.'
    - id: syverson2004c
      title: 'Market Structure and Productivity: A Concrete Example'
      authors: C. Syverson
      year: 2004
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 112(6): ready-mixed concrete markets with denser construction activity have higher lower-bound and average plant productivity and less dispersion.'
    - id: bloom2013
      title: Does Management Matter? Evidence from India
      authors: N. Bloom, B. Eifert, A. Mahajan, D. McKenzie, J. Roberts
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 128(1): firm growth and reallocation to better-managed firms were constrained by limited managerial (owner) time.'

    text readout "Town: {avg:1}\nloaves per baker" #2f6b8f x 790 y 540 font_size 38 hidden true
    scratch sc cites ["bhs2013"] work ["vs workers spread at random:","US: +50%","W. Europe: +20–30%","E. Europe, early 1990s: ≈ 0, then rising"] x 785 y 330 font_size 22 hidden true
    text comp "competition\ndoes the moving" #3d3833 cites ["syverson2004c"] x 780 y 320 font_size 34 hidden true
    text time "the owner's time\nruns out" #b5482e cites ["bloom2013"] x 780 y 320 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mx work ["θ = share of bakers, A = loaves per baker",{"tex":"\\sum_i \\theta_i A_i = \\overline{A} + \\sum_i (\\theta_i-\\overline{\\theta})(A_i-\\overline{A})"},{"tex":"87.5 = 75 + (0.25)(25) + (-0.25)(-25)"},"2nd term: who gets the workers"] x 500 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Who gets the workers"

Better management lifts a firm. But a country's productivity also depends on something else: which firms get the workers.
    draw axes

To see it, take two bakeries on the mainland, wit (@draw bar_1 bar_2@)h illustrative numbers. Dockside makes a hundred loaves per baker a day, Seaview fifty. Twenty bakers, ten in each.

The town  (@draw readout@)makes seventy-five loaves per baker.

Now move five bakers to Dockside. Neither bakery changed a thing, and the town jumps to eighty-seven and a half.
    animate m 15 duration 2.5 ghost true

Try it: slide bakers between the two and watch the town's number.
    explore params ["m"]

    animate m 15 duration 0.5

B: Economists call that reallocation. And they'v (@draw sc_box sc_line_1@)e measured how much the real thing is worth.

B: In American manufacturing, labour prod (@draw sc_line_2@)uctivity is about fifty percent higher than if workers were spread across firms at random.

B: In Western Eu (@draw sc_line_3@)rope, twenty to thirty. In Eastern Europe at the start of t (@draw sc_line_4@)he 1990s, close to nothing; then it grew as markets opened.

    erase sc

And competition d (@draw comp@)oes the moving. Where buyers can easily switch, as in ready-mixed concrete in busy markets, the weakest plants are squeezed out and the floor rises.

    erase comp

B: Then why not send all twenty bakers to Dockside? Because a good man (@draw time@)ager's time runs out. In the Indian firms, the owners' own hours capped how far the best could grow.

    erase time

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a neat bit of algebra behind that fifty percent.

@math

    erase mathq

Town productivity is an average of the bakeries, weighted by each one's share of the bakers.
    draw mx_box mx_line_1

Split it in two: the plain average of the firms, plus a term that is big when the bigger shares sit with the better firms.
    draw mx_line_2

For the two bakeries: seventy-five, plus twelve and a half from putting three quarters of the bakers at Dockside.
    draw mx_line_3

That second term is the one measured at fifty percent in America and near zero in early-1990s Eastern Europe.
    draw mx_line_4

    erase mx

@test

    erase mathq

## Measured how?
use: data_table
with:
    columns:
      - ''
      - Loaves/baker
      - Price
      - $/baker
    rows:
      - [Quayside, 80, $2.50, $200]
      - [Hilltop, 50, $4, $200]
    font_size: 32
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 420
      w: 820
      h: 230
sources:
    - id: fhs2008
      title: 'Reallocation, Firm Turnover, and Efficiency: Selection on Productivity or Profitability?'
      authors: L. Foster, J. Haltiwanger, C. Syverson
      year: 2008
      finding: 'American Economic Review 98(1): for eleven homogeneous products (incl. ready-mixed concrete, white pan bread, raw cane sugar, corrugated boxes) physical productivity (TFPQ) is more dispersed than revenue productivity and is negatively correlated with plant prices.'
    - id: syverson2011
      title: What Determines Productivity?
      authors: C. Syverson
      year: 2011
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Literature 49(2): quantity-based TFP is more dispersed than revenue-based TFP because more efficient producers sell at lower prices; revenue measures understate physical efficiency differences.'
    - id: bkr2021
      title: Misallocation or Mismeasurement?
      authors: M. Bils, P. J. Klenow, C. Ruane
      year: 2021
      finding: 'Journal of Monetary Economics 124: correcting for measurement error lowers the potential gains from reallocation in Indian manufacturing (1985–2013) by about 20% (in the US by about 60%).'
    - id: bloom2013
      title: Does Management Matter? Evidence from India
      authors: N. Bloom, B. Eifert, A. Mahajan, D. McKenzie, J. Roberts
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 128(1): randomised management consulting in 17 Indian textile firms (28 plants).'
    - id: bloomvr2007
      title: Measuring and Explaining Management Practices Across Firms and Countries
      authors: N. Bloom, J. Van Reenen
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 122(4): management scores higher with more product-market competition, lower in family firms run by the eldest son (primogeniture).'

    text gapnote "loaves: +60%   ·   dollars: equal" #b5482e x 500 y 370 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c1 "physical gaps: even bigger" #3d3833 cites ["fhs2008","syverson2011"] x 500 y 290 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c2 "India: gains −20% once errors fixed" #3d3833 cites ["bkr2021"] x 500 y 290 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c3 "causal proof: 17 firms" #3d3833 cites ["bloom2013"] x 500 y 290 font_size 36 hidden true
    text k1 "big, lasting gaps: consensus" #2f6b8f x 500 y 310 font_size 34 hidden true
    text k2 "management a real cause: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 500 y 255 font_size 34 hidden true
    text k3 "how much is fixable: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 200 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards srt check each hidden true
        Narrows the gap: Imports compete, Prices easy to compare
        Widens the gap: New rivals need a licence, Eldest son inherits by custom, Permit cap on growth
    text end "how it's run  +  who gets to grow" #3d3833 x 500 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Measured how?"

B: But wait. How do we know the top plants really make  (@draw header@)more? Most data record sales in dollars, not loaves.

Take two more bakeries, with illustrat (@draw row_1@)ive numbers. Quayside bakes eighty loaves per baker and sells them at two dollars fifty.

Hilltop, alon (@draw row_2@)e on its hill, bakes fifty and charges four dollars.

In dollars both make two hu (@draw gapnote@)ndred per baker. In loaves, Quayside makes sixty percent more.

Where researchers counted physical units, in concrete, sug (@draw c1@)ar, even white bread, the gaps were even bigger: efficient plants tend to charge less.

    erase c1

B: But data can mislead the other way too. Correcting for erro (@draw c2@)rs in India's factory records cut the estimated gains from reallocation by about a fifth.

    erase c2

B: And the clean causal pro (@draw c3@)of of management is one experiment, with seventeen firms.

    erase c3

How sure are we? That firms in the same ind (@draw k1@)ustry differ hugely, and lastingly: consensus. That management i (@draw k2@)s a real part of it: mostly agreed.

B: How much of the gap is waste a better economy coul (@draw k3@)d fix, and how much is mismeasurement: contested.

    erase k1 k2 k3 gapnote

    quiz "A country lets failing state banks keep lending to their oldest clients, however weak. What happens to the gap between its best and worst firms?" voice b
        intro: Three questions to try it out.
        right: It widens: cheap credit keeps the weak tail alive, and holds its workers there.
        wrong: Ask who keeps the workers when weak firms can't fail: here, the gap widens.
        + It widens
        * It narrows
        * No change

    hide header row_1 row_2

    draw srt

    ask "Does each of these narrow or widen the gap between an industry's best and worst firms? Sort them." on srt store f stage own
        right: All five: competition squeezes the weak tail; protection fattens it.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: imports, prices narrow it.

    erase srt

    show header row_1 row_2

    quiz "A cement plant in a remote valley has the highest revenue per worker in its industry. What should you ask first?" voice b
        right: Its price: with no rival near, high revenue may be a high price, not more cement per worker.
        wrong: Revenue is quantity times price: ask whether no rival is near.
        + Does it charge more because no rival is near?
        * Does it pay its workers more?
        * Is it older than the others?

    erase header row_1 row_2

So why are some firms so much more productive? Partly how (@draw end@) they are run, and partly whether competition lets the good ones grow. How new firms are born, and why most die, is the next question.

